                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
          %h3#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The identity
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mn 1
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi P
                      %mo :
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi P
                      %mo →
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1_{\mathbf{P}}: \mathbf{P} \to \mathbf{P}
                carries a club structure. The multiplication
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi μ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
                of the club, on the object level, is given by the assignment
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo ;
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ↦
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mn 1
                      %mo +
                      %mi …
                      %mo +
                      %msub
                        %mi k
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n; k_1, \ldots, k_n) \mapsto k_1 + \ldots + k_n
              %p
                and on morphisms, it is given by the operad structure on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Aut
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mi Lin
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Aut(n) = Lin(n)
                discussed above. Algebras over this club are symmetric strict monoidal categories. Algebras in the “pseudo” sense over the induced 2-monad on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unbiased"} unbiased
                non-strict
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+categories"} symmetric monoidal categories
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1)
                is the free (biased, non-strict) symmetric monoidal category on one generator, then the symmetric monoidal equivalence
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo :
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma: F(1) \to \mathbf{P}
                carries a club structure whose strict algebras are (biased, non-strict) symmetric monoidal categories.
            %li
              %p
                Let
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}
                be the
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/braid+category"} braid category
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo :
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mo →
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma: \mathbf{B} \to \mathbf{P}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
